{{right|''Shak.'', M. N. D., iv. 2. 4.}}}}

4. To carry into banishment, as a criminal to a penal colony; carry beyond seas.

{{smaller block|But we generally make a shift to return after being ''transported'', and are ten times greater rogues than before, and much more cunning.
{{right|''Swift'', Last Speech of Ebenezer Elliston.}}}}

{{smaller block|And never mind what Felix says, for he 's so masterful he'd stay in prison and be ''transported'' whether or no, only to have his own way. ''George Eliot'', Felix Holt, xxxviii.}}

5. To carry away by strong emotion, as joy or anger; carry out of one's self; render beside one's self.

{{smaller block|The hearts of men, . . .
''Transported'' with celestiall desyre
Of those faire formes, may lift themselves up hyer.
{{right|''Spenser'', Hymn of Heavenly Beauty, l. 18.}}}}

{{smaller block|Oh, my joys!
Whither will you ''transport'' me?
{{right|''Beau. and Fl.'', Knight of Burning Pestle, iii. 1.}}}}

'''transport''' (trȧns′pōrt), ''n.'' [< F. ''transport'' = Sp. ''transporte'', ''trasporte'' = Pg. ''transporte''; from the verb.] 1. Transportation; carriage; conveyance.

{{smaller block|The Romans . . . stipulated with the Carthaginians to furnish them with ships both for ''transport'' and war.
{{right|''Arbuthnot'', Ancient Coins, p. 239.}}}}

{{smaller block|The ''transport'' of blocks by ice in rivers of cold climates has often been described. ''Prestwich'', Geology, i. 190.}}

2†. Transformation; alteration.

{{smaller block|Many are now poor wandering beggars . . . who are descended of the blood and lineage of great kings and emperors, occasioned . . . by the ''transport'' and revolutions of kingdoms and empires. ''Urquhart'', tr. of Rabelais, i. 2.}}

3. A ship or vessel employed by government

readings.c1s3:
for carrying soldiers, warlike stores, or provisions from one place to another, or to convey convicts to the place of their destination.

{{smaller block|Grant organized an expedition to counteract this design, and on the evening of November 6 left Cairo with about 3000 men on ''transports'', under convoy of 2 gun-boats, and steamed down the river.|{{right|''The Century'', XXXVI. 575.}}}}

4. A convict transported or sentenced to exile.

{{smaller block|If he had been a ''transport'' he could not have been treated worse. He told his father that he was driving him on the road to transportation.|{{right|''Mayhew'', London Labour and London Poor, I. 470.}}}}

5. Vehement emotion; passion; rapture; ecstasy.

{{smaller block|In the afternoone I went againe with my Wife to the Dutchess of Newcastle, who receiv'd her in a kind of ''transport'', suitable to her extravagant humour and dresse.|{{right|''Evelyn'', Diary, April 27, 1667.}}}}

{{smaller block|I broke open my letter in a ''transport'' of joy.|{{right|''Addison'', A Friend of Mankind.}}}}

'''Transport screw.''' {{smaller|See ''screw''<sup>1</sup>.}}

'''transportability''' (trȧns-pōr-ta-bil′i-ti), ''n.'' [< ''transportable'' + ''-ity'' (see ''-bility'').] The character of being transportable; the capacity of being transported.

'''transportable''' (trȧns-pōr′ta-bl), ''a.'' [= F. ''transportable'' = It. ''trasportabile''; as ''transport'' + ''-able''.] 1. Capable of being transported.

{{smaller block|The direct result of a union of two or more distinct protoplasmic masses, in plant life, is a condensed, inactive, and ''transportable'' condition of the life of the species—that is, a seed or spore.|{{right|''Amer. Nat.'', June, 1890, p. 577.}}}}

readings.c2s1:
¶
2. Involving transportation; subjecting to transportation.

{{smaller block|The statute 7 Geo. II. c. 21 . . . makes it a felony (''transportable'' for seven years) unlawfully and maliciously to assault another with any offensive weapon or instrument, . . . with a felonious intent to rob.
{{right|''Blackstone'', Com., IV. xvii.}}}}

'''transportage†''' (trȧns-pōr′tāj), ''n.'' [< ''transport'' + ''-age''.] Transportation.

{{smaller block|Here be my keyes, my trunks take to thy charge;<br>Such gold fit for ''transportage'' as I have<br>Ile beare along.
{{right|''Heywood'', Fair Maid of the West (Works, ed. 1874, II. 273).}}}}

'''transportal''' (trȧns-pōr′tal), ''n.'' [< ''transport'' + ''-al''.] The act of removal from one locality to another; transportation.

{{smaller block|The relative length of these organs [pistils and stamens] is an adaptation for the safe ''transportal'' by insects of the pollen from the one form to the other.
{{right|''Darwin'', Different Forms of Flowers, p. 253.}}}}

'''transportance†''' (trȧns-pōr′ta̤ns), ''n.'' [< ''transport'' + ''-ance''.] Conveyance.

{{smaller block|O, be thou my Charon,<br>And give me swift ''transportance'' to those fields<br>Where I may wallow in the lily-beds<br>Proposed for the deserver!
{{right|''Shak.'', T. and C., iii. 2. 12.}}}}

'''transportant†''' (trȧns-pōr′ta̤nt), ''a.'' [< ''transport'' + ''-ant''.] Transporting; ravishing.

{{smaller block|So rapturous a joy, and ''transportant'' love.
{{right|''Dr. H. More'', Mystery of Godliness, p. 227. (''Latham.'')}}}}

readings.c2s2:
¶
'''transportation''' (trȧns-pōr-tā′shọn), ''n.'' [< F. ''transportation'' = Pr. ''transportacio'' = Sp. ''transportacion, trasportacion'' = Pg. ''transportação'' = It. ''trasportazione'', < L. ''transportatio''(''n''-), a removing, transporting, < ''transportare'', pp. ''transportatus'', remove, transport: see ''transport''.] 1. The act of transporting, or conveying from one place to another, or the state of being so transported; carriage; conveyance; transmission.

{{smaller block|There may be ''transportation'' and isolation of very small fragments of a very variable species.
{{right|''Amer. Jour. Sci.'', XL. 9.}}}}

2. The removal or banishment, for a specified term, of a convict to a penal settlement in another country. {{smaller|The transportation of persons convicted of crime prevails in France and Russia, but in Great Britain it is now superseded by penal servitude. See ''penal''.}}

3. Transport; ecstasy; rapture.

{{smaller block|She did bite her lips in pronouncing the words softly to herself; sometimes she would smile, and her eyes would sparkle with a sudden ''transportation''.
{{right|''History of Francion'' (1655). (''Nares''.)}}}}

{{smaller block|All pleasures that affect the body must needs weary, because they transport; and all ''transportation'' is a violence, and no violence can be lasting.
{{right|''Steele'', Tatler, No. 211.}}}}

4. Means of transporting, as wagons or other vehicles; also, the cost of traveling. [U. S.]

{{smaller block|A lot of miscellaneous ''transportation'', composed of riding-horses, ambulances, and other vehicles, which, over roads rendered almost impassable by mud, made their progress to the last degree vexatious and toilsome.
{{right|''The Century'', XXXIX. 564.}}}}

readings.c3s2:
¶
'''transposal''' (träns-pō′za̤l), ''n.'' [< ''transpose'' + ''-al''.] The act of transposing, or the state of being transposed; transposition. ''Swift'', Tale of a Tub, Pref.

'''transpose''' (träns-pōz′), ''v. t.''; pret. and pp. ''transposed'', ppr. ''transposing''. [< ME. ''transposen'', < OF. (and F.) ''transposer'', transpose; cf. Sp. ''transponer'', ''trasponer'' = Pg. ''traspor'' = It. ''trasponere'', ''trasporre'', < L. ''transponere'', set over, remove, < ''trans'', over, + ''ponere'', place: see ''ponent'' and ''pose''<sup>2</sup>.] '''1'''†. To remove to a different place; transfer; transport.

{{smaller block|So many other nations of the world haue beene ''transposed'' and forced to flie from one region to another.
{{right|''Verstegan'', Rest. of Decayed Intelligence (ed. 1628), p. 43.}}
Bethink you of a place
You may ''transpose'' her.
{{right|''Shirley'', Maid's Revenge, iii. 1.}}}}

'''2'''. To cause (two or, less frequently, more objects) to change places.

{{smaller block|"This infant was called John Little," quoth he;
"Which name shall be changed anon;
The words we'll ''transpose''; so, wherever he goes,
His name shall be call'd Little John."
{{right|''Robin Hood and Little John'' (Child's Ballads, V. 222).}}}}

'''3'''. In ''alg.'', to bring, as any term of an equation, over from one side to the other side. See ''transposition'', 2.—'''4'''. In ''rhet.'', to change the usual order of (words).—'''5'''. In ''music'', to alter the tonality of (a piece or passage) from a given tonality, either in performance or in transcription. See ''transposition'', 4.—'''6'''†. To transform.

{{smaller block|That which you are my thoughts cannot ''transpose'';
Angels are bright still, though the brightest fell.
{{right|''Shak.'', Macbeth, iv. 3. 21.}}}}
